1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,782
The biggest cruise ships rise more than

2
00:00:02,782 --> 00:00:04,850
sixty metres above the water,

3
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:07,418
as tall as a twenty storey building.

4
00:00:07,598 --> 00:00:09,235
Yet below the water,

5
00:00:09,235 --> 00:00:12,018
the hull goes down only about nine

6
00:00:12,018 --> 00:00:12,591
metres.

7
00:00:12,771 --> 00:00:14,129
It looks impossible.

8
00:00:14,309 --> 00:00:16,954
A tall, thin tower floating on a

9
00:00:16,954 --> 00:00:20,177
shallow base should simply fall over in

10
00:00:20,177 --> 00:00:21,995
the first strong wind.

11
00:00:22,175 --> 00:00:23,092
It does not.

12
00:00:23,272 --> 00:00:26,648
Today, ten parts of a cruise ship,

13
00:00:26,648 --> 00:00:29,030
and why each one exists.

14
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:31,538
The secret is not what you see

15
00:00:31,538 --> 00:00:32,689
above the water.

16
00:00:32,869 --> 00:00:34,751
It is where the weight is,

17
00:00:34,751 --> 00:00:36,270
how wide the hull is,

18
00:00:36,270 --> 00:00:38,513
and a long list of systems that

19
00:00:38,513 --> 00:00:39,744
fight every wave,

20
00:00:39,744 --> 00:00:42,204
every gust of wind and every turn.

21
00:00:42,384 --> 00:00:45,277
A cruise ship is a balancing act,

22
00:00:45,277 --> 00:00:48,258
engineered down to the last tonne.

23
00:00:48,608 --> 00:00:51,768
As always, every fact here comes from

24
00:00:51,768 --> 00:00:53,049
a named source,

25
00:00:53,049 --> 00:00:54,928
maritime safety rules,

26
00:00:54,928 --> 00:00:57,831
shipbuilders and naval references,

27
00:00:57,831 --> 00:01:00,393
all listed in the description.

28
00:01:00,573 --> 00:01:01,989
Let's start with the weight.

29
00:01:02,339 --> 00:01:04,919
Why doesn't a tall cruise ship tip

30
00:01:04,919 --> 00:01:05,299
over?

31
00:01:05,479 --> 00:01:08,192
Because almost all of its heavy parts

32
00:01:08,192 --> 00:01:09,879
are at the very bottom.

33
00:01:10,059 --> 00:01:11,934
The engines, generators,

34
00:01:11,934 --> 00:01:14,825
fuel, fresh water tanks and the thick

35
00:01:14,825 --> 00:01:17,169
steel of the hull all sit low,

36
00:01:17,169 --> 00:01:18,732
below the waterline.

37
00:01:18,912 --> 00:01:21,455
The decks above are full of cabins,

38
00:01:21,455 --> 00:01:22,836
theatres and pools,

39
00:01:22,836 --> 00:01:25,379
which are big but relatively light.

40
00:01:25,729 --> 00:01:28,395
The result is a low centre of

41
00:01:28,395 --> 00:01:29,130
gravity.

42
00:01:29,310 --> 00:01:30,768
When the ship leans,

43
00:01:30,768 --> 00:01:33,464
the heavy bottom swings back like the

44
00:01:33,464 --> 00:01:35,942
weight in a toy that always stands

45
00:01:35,942 --> 00:01:36,160
up.

46
00:01:36,340 --> 00:01:40,666
Designers often use lighter materials like aluminium

47
00:01:40,666 --> 00:01:42,912
high up, to keep the top as

48
00:01:42,912 --> 00:01:44,409
light as possible.

49
00:01:44,589 --> 00:01:46,851
Every tonne added on a top deck

50
00:01:46,851 --> 00:01:49,260
has to be balanced by weight down

51
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:49,697
below.

52
00:01:50,048 --> 00:01:52,940
Naval architects describe this with a number

53
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:54,912
called the metacentric height.

54
00:01:55,092 --> 00:01:58,046
It measures how strongly a ship pushes

55
00:01:58,046 --> 00:02:00,688
itself back upright when it tilts.

56
00:02:00,868 --> 00:02:03,997
Too small, and the ship rolls over

57
00:02:03,997 --> 00:02:04,641
easily.

58
00:02:04,821 --> 00:02:07,695
Too large, and the ship snaps back

59
00:02:07,695 --> 00:02:10,568
so fast that passengers feel sick.

60
00:02:10,748 --> 00:02:13,431
Cruise ships are tuned for a gentle,

61
00:02:13,431 --> 00:02:14,847
comfortable middle.

62
00:02:15,197 --> 00:02:17,666
Why are cruise ships so wide and

63
00:02:17,666 --> 00:02:18,900
flat underneath?

64
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:21,414
Because width gives stability.

65
00:02:21,594 --> 00:02:23,385
When a wide ship tilts,

66
00:02:23,385 --> 00:02:26,188
the side going down pushes deep into

67
00:02:26,188 --> 00:02:28,991
the water, and the water pushes back

68
00:02:28,991 --> 00:02:30,626
up hard on that side.

69
00:02:30,806 --> 00:02:34,126
A wide, boxy hull resists tilting much

70
00:02:34,126 --> 00:02:36,573
more strongly than a narrow,

71
00:02:36,573 --> 00:02:37,621
rounded one.

72
00:02:37,972 --> 00:02:40,200
The tall sides of a cruise ship

73
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:42,499
also catch the wind like a sail.

74
00:02:42,679 --> 00:02:45,585
A strong side wind can push the

75
00:02:45,585 --> 00:02:47,834
ship over a few degrees.

76
00:02:48,014 --> 00:02:50,879
Designers calculate the wind force on the

77
00:02:50,879 --> 00:02:51,997
whole side area,

78
00:02:51,997 --> 00:02:54,094
and make sure the hull is wide

79
00:02:54,094 --> 00:02:56,539
enough and the weight low enough to

80
00:02:56,539 --> 00:02:59,125
resist it with a large safety margin.

81
00:02:59,475 --> 00:03:02,389
The wide hull also creates more space

82
00:03:02,389 --> 00:03:04,438
for cabins with balconies,

83
00:03:04,438 --> 00:03:06,092
which customers love.

84
00:03:06,272 --> 00:03:08,668
But the width is limited by the

85
00:03:08,668 --> 00:03:11,141
canals and ports a ship needs to

86
00:03:11,141 --> 00:03:11,450
use.

87
00:03:11,630 --> 00:03:14,325
Ships built to pass the Panama Canal

88
00:03:14,325 --> 00:03:15,821
had a maximum width.

89
00:03:16,001 --> 00:03:18,957
The newest giants were designed for the

90
00:03:18,957 --> 00:03:20,473
larger, newer locks,

91
00:03:20,473 --> 00:03:22,747
or to skip the canal entirely.

92
00:03:23,097 --> 00:03:25,423
Why do cruise ships have hidden wings

93
00:03:25,423 --> 00:03:26,429
under the water?

94
00:03:26,609 --> 00:03:28,768
They are stabiliser fins.

95
00:03:28,948 --> 00:03:30,843
Each side of the hull has a

96
00:03:30,843 --> 00:03:33,650
fin that slides out below the waterline.

97
00:03:33,830 --> 00:03:36,106
When a wave starts to roll the

98
00:03:36,106 --> 00:03:37,395
ship to one side,

99
00:03:37,395 --> 00:03:38,457
the fins tilt,

100
00:03:38,457 --> 00:03:40,429
like the wings of a plane,

101
00:03:40,429 --> 00:03:43,236
and the flowing water pushes the ship

102
00:03:43,236 --> 00:03:44,070
back level.

103
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:47,514
Sensors measure the roll many times per

104
00:03:47,514 --> 00:03:50,449
second, and a computer moves the fins

105
00:03:50,449 --> 00:03:53,464
to fight it before passengers feel it.

106
00:03:53,644 --> 00:03:55,661
The fins need the ship to be

107
00:03:55,661 --> 00:03:56,741
moving to work,

108
00:03:56,741 --> 00:03:59,623
because they depend on the water flowing

109
00:03:59,623 --> 00:03:59,983
past.

110
00:04:00,163 --> 00:04:03,092
Newer designs can also work at very

111
00:04:03,092 --> 00:04:06,106
low speed, by flapping like the fins

112
00:04:06,106 --> 00:04:06,943
of a fish.

113
00:04:07,293 --> 00:04:09,510
The fins fold back into the hull

114
00:04:09,510 --> 00:04:11,242
when they are not needed,

115
00:04:11,242 --> 00:04:13,599
to reduce drag and protect them in

116
00:04:13,599 --> 00:04:13,945
port.

117
00:04:14,125 --> 00:04:16,969
Stabilisers do not stop a ship from

118
00:04:16,969 --> 00:04:18,026
tipping over.

119
00:04:18,206 --> 00:04:20,226
The hull and the low weight do

120
00:04:20,226 --> 00:04:20,563
that.

121
00:04:20,743 --> 00:04:22,805
The fins are there for comfort,

122
00:04:22,805 --> 00:04:24,800
so a dinner does not slide off

123
00:04:24,800 --> 00:04:26,397
the table in rough seas.

124
00:04:26,747 --> 00:04:29,377
Why do ships pump water from one

125
00:04:29,377 --> 00:04:30,856
side to the other?

126
00:04:31,036 --> 00:04:32,209
To stay upright.

127
00:04:32,389 --> 00:04:33,804
When a ship turns,

128
00:04:33,804 --> 00:04:35,141
it leans outward.

129
00:04:35,321 --> 00:04:39,123
When thousands of passengers gather on one

130
00:04:39,123 --> 00:04:41,024
side to watch a view,

131
00:04:41,024 --> 00:04:43,559
or cargo is loaded unevenly,

132
00:04:43,559 --> 00:04:44,736
it leans too.

133
00:04:44,916 --> 00:04:47,788
Anti heeling tanks on each side move

134
00:04:47,788 --> 00:04:50,659
water across the ship to cancel that

135
00:04:50,659 --> 00:04:51,058
lean.

136
00:04:51,408 --> 00:04:53,451
The system is automatic.

137
00:04:53,631 --> 00:04:55,452
Sensors measure the tilt,

138
00:04:55,452 --> 00:04:58,073
and pumps move hundreds of tonnes of

139
00:04:58,073 --> 00:05:00,841
water to the opposite side in minutes.

140
00:05:01,021 --> 00:05:04,067
On ferries, where trucks drive on and

141
00:05:04,067 --> 00:05:05,138
off one side,

142
00:05:05,138 --> 00:05:07,278
the tanks work constantly.

143
00:05:07,458 --> 00:05:08,764
On cruise ships,

144
00:05:08,764 --> 00:05:11,458
they keep the decks level in port

145
00:05:11,458 --> 00:05:12,845
and during turns.

146
00:05:13,196 --> 00:05:15,471
The same tanks are part of the

147
00:05:15,471 --> 00:05:17,139
ship's ballast system,

148
00:05:17,139 --> 00:05:19,945
which also adds or removes weight low

149
00:05:19,945 --> 00:05:22,068
down to keep the ship at the

150
00:05:22,068 --> 00:05:24,571
right depth as fuel and water are

151
00:05:24,571 --> 00:05:26,315
used during the voyage.

152
00:05:26,495 --> 00:05:30,294
A cruise ship is constantly adjusting itself,

153
00:05:30,294 --> 00:05:33,333
like a person balancing on one foot.

154
00:05:33,682 --> 00:05:36,528
Why do cruise ships have heavy sliding

155
00:05:36,528 --> 00:05:39,000
steel doors deep inside the hull?

156
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:40,782
They are watertight doors.

157
00:05:40,962 --> 00:05:44,776
The lower hull is divided into compartments

158
00:05:44,776 --> 00:05:47,615
by steel walls called bulkheads.

159
00:05:47,795 --> 00:05:49,472
If the hull is damaged,

160
00:05:49,472 --> 00:05:50,638
the doors close,

161
00:05:50,638 --> 00:05:52,971
and the water is trapped in only

162
00:05:52,971 --> 00:05:54,357
a few compartments,

163
00:05:54,357 --> 00:05:56,908
instead of flooding the whole ship.

164
00:05:57,259 --> 00:05:59,808
The rules for this come largely from

165
00:05:59,808 --> 00:06:02,498
the sinking of the Titanic in nineteen

166
00:06:02,498 --> 00:06:02,994
twelve.

167
00:06:03,174 --> 00:06:05,875
Its compartments were not tall enough,

168
00:06:05,875 --> 00:06:08,293
and water spilled over from one to

169
00:06:08,293 --> 00:06:08,932
the next.

170
00:06:09,112 --> 00:06:13,176
The first international convention on the safety

171
00:06:13,176 --> 00:06:14,446
of life at sea,

172
00:06:14,446 --> 00:06:15,716
known as SOLAS,

173
00:06:15,716 --> 00:06:17,579
was agreed soon after,

174
00:06:17,579 --> 00:06:20,119
and it is still updated today.

175
00:06:20,469 --> 00:06:23,533
Modern rules require a passenger ship to

176
00:06:23,533 --> 00:06:26,673
stay afloat and upright even with several

177
00:06:26,673 --> 00:06:28,282
compartments flooded.

178
00:06:28,462 --> 00:06:30,535
The doors can be closed from the

179
00:06:30,535 --> 00:06:31,701
bridge in seconds.

180
00:06:31,881 --> 00:06:33,323
When they slide shut,

181
00:06:33,323 --> 00:06:36,070
they are strong enough to crush anything

182
00:06:36,070 --> 00:06:36,963
in their way,

183
00:06:36,963 --> 00:06:39,366
which is why crew are trained never

184
00:06:39,366 --> 00:06:41,564
to pass through them carelessly.

185
00:06:41,914 --> 00:06:44,412
Why do the propellers of many cruise

186
00:06:44,412 --> 00:06:46,632
ships hang in pods that can turn

187
00:06:46,632 --> 00:06:47,951
all the way around?

188
00:06:48,131 --> 00:06:50,406
They are azimuth pods,

189
00:06:50,406 --> 00:06:52,577
often called azipods.

190
00:06:52,757 --> 00:06:55,563
Each pod holds an electric motor and

191
00:06:55,563 --> 00:06:58,213
a propeller, and the whole pod can

192
00:06:58,213 --> 00:07:00,083
rotate in any direction.

193
00:07:00,263 --> 00:07:02,063
There is no need for a separate

194
00:07:02,063 --> 00:07:02,469
rudder.

195
00:07:02,820 --> 00:07:05,899
Turning the pod points the thrust exactly

196
00:07:05,899 --> 00:07:07,927
where the captain wants it,

197
00:07:07,927 --> 00:07:10,331
forwards, sideways or backwards.

198
00:07:10,511 --> 00:07:13,197
Combined with bow thrusters,

199
00:07:13,197 --> 00:07:16,841
a giant cruise ship can slide sideways

200
00:07:16,841 --> 00:07:17,992
into a dock,

201
00:07:17,992 --> 00:07:19,622
spin on the spot,

202
00:07:19,622 --> 00:07:21,541
or stop much faster.

203
00:07:21,721 --> 00:07:25,124
Many cruise ships can dock without tugboats,

204
00:07:25,124 --> 00:07:27,909
saving time and money in every port.

205
00:07:28,259 --> 00:07:29,961
The motors are electric,

206
00:07:29,961 --> 00:07:32,728
powered by generators deep in the hull.

207
00:07:32,908 --> 00:07:35,547
That means the engines do not have

208
00:07:35,547 --> 00:07:38,341
to be connected to the propellers by

209
00:07:38,341 --> 00:07:41,445
long shafts, so designers can place them

210
00:07:41,445 --> 00:07:44,549
wherever the weight and space work best,

211
00:07:44,549 --> 00:07:47,653
usually low, which also helps stability.

212
00:07:47,833 --> 00:07:50,822
Cruise ships began using pods in the

213
00:07:50,822 --> 00:07:52,732
late nineteen nineties.

214
00:07:53,082 --> 00:07:55,742
Why do modern cruise ships have two

215
00:07:55,742 --> 00:07:57,413
separate engine rooms?

216
00:07:57,593 --> 00:08:00,215
Because of a rule called safe return

217
00:08:00,215 --> 00:08:00,797
to port.

218
00:08:00,977 --> 00:08:04,372
For large passenger ships built since two

219
00:08:04,372 --> 00:08:07,022
thousand ten, a fire or flood in

220
00:08:07,022 --> 00:08:09,671
one area must not leave the ship

221
00:08:09,671 --> 00:08:10,416
helpless.

222
00:08:10,596 --> 00:08:12,781
The ship must be able to sail

223
00:08:12,781 --> 00:08:14,589
back to port on its own,

224
00:08:14,589 --> 00:08:17,377
with essential systems still working.

225
00:08:17,726 --> 00:08:18,572
To do that,

226
00:08:18,572 --> 00:08:21,723
the ship is designed with doubled systems

227
00:08:21,723 --> 00:08:23,183
in separate spaces.

228
00:08:23,363 --> 00:08:25,745
Two or more engine rooms,

229
00:08:25,745 --> 00:08:29,460
separate switchboards, backup steering,

230
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:33,462
and areas where passengers can stay safely

231
00:08:33,462 --> 00:08:35,081
with food, water,

232
00:08:35,081 --> 00:08:36,796
toilets and light.

233
00:08:36,976 --> 00:08:38,659
If one side is lost,

234
00:08:38,659 --> 00:08:41,353
the other keeps the ship moving.

235
00:08:41,702 --> 00:08:43,421
The idea is simple.

236
00:08:43,601 --> 00:08:44,973
On the open ocean,

237
00:08:44,973 --> 00:08:48,022
the safest place for thousands of people

238
00:08:48,022 --> 00:08:49,928
is often the ship itself.

239
00:08:50,108 --> 00:08:54,003
Evacuating a giant ship into lifeboats is

240
00:08:54,003 --> 00:08:55,333
a last resort.

241
00:08:55,513 --> 00:08:58,126
So engineers design the ship to be

242
00:08:58,126 --> 00:08:59,433
its own lifeboat,

243
00:08:59,433 --> 00:09:02,353
able to bring everyone home even after

244
00:09:02,353 --> 00:09:03,814
a serious accident.

245
00:09:04,163 --> 00:09:07,763
Why are lifeboats almost always bright orange?

246
00:09:07,943 --> 00:09:09,313
So they can be seen.

247
00:09:09,493 --> 00:09:10,416
In a grey,

248
00:09:10,416 --> 00:09:13,277
rough sea, a small boat is very

249
00:09:13,277 --> 00:09:15,769
hard to spot from a ship or

250
00:09:15,769 --> 00:09:16,969
a helicopter.

251
00:09:17,149 --> 00:09:20,752
A strong orange colour stands out against

252
00:09:20,752 --> 00:09:24,004
water, sky and fog better than almost

253
00:09:24,004 --> 00:09:25,497
any other colour,

254
00:09:25,497 --> 00:09:27,870
which is why it is used for

255
00:09:27,870 --> 00:09:31,824
lifeboats, life jackets and rescue equipment.

256
00:09:32,175 --> 00:09:33,462
On cruise ships,

257
00:09:33,462 --> 00:09:36,036
the lifeboats hang on the sides,

258
00:09:36,036 --> 00:09:38,289
a few decks above the water,

259
00:09:38,289 --> 00:09:41,346
on davits, the cranes that lower them.

260
00:09:41,526 --> 00:09:43,524
They are fully enclosed,

261
00:09:43,524 --> 00:09:45,106
with engines, food,

262
00:09:45,106 --> 00:09:46,437
water and seats.

263
00:09:46,617 --> 00:09:49,853
The largest modern lifeboats can carry over

264
00:09:49,853 --> 00:09:52,562
three hundred and fifty people each.

265
00:09:52,912 --> 00:09:56,444
The rules require enough lifeboat and life

266
00:09:56,444 --> 00:09:59,219
raft space for everyone on board,

267
00:09:59,219 --> 00:10:00,145
plus extra.

268
00:10:00,325 --> 00:10:02,979
Every passenger takes part in a safety

269
00:10:02,979 --> 00:10:05,354
drill before the ship leaves port,

270
00:10:05,354 --> 00:10:08,287
and the crew practises launching the boats

271
00:10:08,287 --> 00:10:08,986
regularly.

272
00:10:09,166 --> 00:10:10,892
They are the part of the ship

273
00:10:10,892 --> 00:10:12,916
everyone hopes will never be used.

274
00:10:13,266 --> 00:10:15,906
Why do engineers tilt a brand new

275
00:10:15,906 --> 00:10:18,467
ship on purpose before it sails?

276
00:10:18,647 --> 00:10:21,375
It is called the inclining experiment.

277
00:10:21,555 --> 00:10:24,163
Known heavy weights are moved from one

278
00:10:24,163 --> 00:10:26,222
side of the deck to the other,

279
00:10:26,222 --> 00:10:29,036
and the engineers measure exactly how far

280
00:10:29,036 --> 00:10:30,065
the ship leans,

281
00:10:30,065 --> 00:10:33,154
using long pendulums hanging inside the ship.

282
00:10:33,504 --> 00:10:35,617
From the size of the weights,

283
00:10:35,617 --> 00:10:37,365
the distance they moved,

284
00:10:37,365 --> 00:10:39,260
and the angle of the tilt,

285
00:10:39,260 --> 00:10:42,539
the engineers calculate where the ship's real

286
00:10:42,539 --> 00:10:44,069
centre of gravity is.

287
00:10:44,249 --> 00:10:46,276
Computers can predict it,

288
00:10:46,276 --> 00:10:48,709
but a real ship always ends up

289
00:10:48,709 --> 00:10:51,709
slightly different from the drawings,

290
00:10:51,709 --> 00:10:53,169
with extra cables,

291
00:10:53,169 --> 00:10:54,790
furniture and paint.

292
00:10:55,141 --> 00:10:57,704
The result decides how much weight the

293
00:10:57,704 --> 00:10:58,715
ship can carry,

294
00:10:58,715 --> 00:10:59,390
and where.

295
00:10:59,570 --> 00:11:02,289
It becomes part of the ship's official

296
00:11:02,289 --> 00:11:05,437
stability book, which the officers use every

297
00:11:05,437 --> 00:11:05,723
day.

298
00:11:05,903 --> 00:11:08,798
A simple experiment with weights and a

299
00:11:08,798 --> 00:11:11,846
pendulum is still the final check before

300
00:11:11,846 --> 00:11:14,436
thousands of people step on board.

301
00:11:14,787 --> 00:11:17,139
Why does a ship lean outward when

302
00:11:17,139 --> 00:11:19,918
it turns, while a bicycle leans inward?

303
00:11:20,098 --> 00:11:22,780
Because of where the forces act.

304
00:11:22,960 --> 00:11:24,474
When a ship turns,

305
00:11:24,474 --> 00:11:27,671
the water pushes sideways on the hull,

306
00:11:27,671 --> 00:11:28,428
low down.

307
00:11:28,608 --> 00:11:29,797
The ship's weight,

308
00:11:29,797 --> 00:11:32,440
higher up, wants to keep going straight.

309
00:11:32,620 --> 00:11:35,314
That pair of forces tips the ship

310
00:11:35,314 --> 00:11:37,844
toward the outside of the turn.

311
00:11:38,195 --> 00:11:39,515
On a fast turn,

312
00:11:39,515 --> 00:11:42,420
a large ship can lean noticeably,

313
00:11:42,420 --> 00:11:45,149
and objects on board can slide.

314
00:11:45,329 --> 00:11:47,827
That is why big ships turn gently,

315
00:11:47,827 --> 00:11:50,325
and why the captain plans turns in

316
00:11:50,325 --> 00:11:50,913
advance.

317
00:11:51,093 --> 00:11:54,506
The stability rules require ships to stay

318
00:11:54,506 --> 00:11:56,754
safe even in a hard turn at

319
00:11:56,754 --> 00:11:59,584
full speed, with a margin for wind

320
00:11:59,584 --> 00:12:00,416
and waves.

321
00:12:00,767 --> 00:12:03,530
Stability is never just one part.

322
00:12:03,710 --> 00:12:05,254
It is the low weight,

323
00:12:05,254 --> 00:12:06,284
the wide hull,

324
00:12:06,284 --> 00:12:07,756
the fins, the tanks,

325
00:12:07,756 --> 00:12:10,845
the compartments and the careful habits of

326
00:12:10,845 --> 00:12:13,125
the crew, all working together.

327
00:12:13,305 --> 00:12:16,602
A cruise ship stays upright because hundreds

328
00:12:16,602 --> 00:12:19,375
of engineers made sure every tonne is

329
00:12:19,375 --> 00:12:21,398
exactly where it should be.

330
00:12:21,748 --> 00:12:23,234
So here are the ten.

331
00:12:23,414 --> 00:12:25,527
Heavy machinery at the bottom.

332
00:12:25,707 --> 00:12:28,307
A wide hull that pushes back.

333
00:12:28,487 --> 00:12:30,391
Hidden fins that fight the roll.

334
00:12:30,571 --> 00:12:33,172
Tanks that move water across the ship.

335
00:12:33,352 --> 00:12:35,720
Steel doors that trap a flood.

336
00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:38,188
Pods that turn all the way around.

337
00:12:38,368 --> 00:12:41,293
Two engine rooms to bring everyone home.

338
00:12:41,473 --> 00:12:43,923
Orange lifeboats that can be seen.

339
00:12:44,103 --> 00:12:46,351
A test that tilts the ship on

340
00:12:46,351 --> 00:12:46,971
purpose.

341
00:12:47,151 --> 00:12:49,287
And the physics of leaning out in

342
00:12:49,287 --> 00:12:49,740
a turn.

343
00:12:50,090 --> 00:12:52,335
A cruise ship looks top heavy,

344
00:12:52,335 --> 00:12:55,103
but it is really a carefully weighted

345
00:12:55,103 --> 00:12:56,974
keel with a hotel on top.

346
00:12:57,154 --> 00:13:00,226
If you want more machines explained part

347
00:13:00,226 --> 00:13:03,145
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

348
00:13:03,325 --> 00:13:05,264
And tell us in the comments,

349
00:13:05,264 --> 00:13:07,481
would you rather sail on a giant

350
00:13:07,481 --> 00:13:09,351
cruise ship or a small one?
